  "pipesName": "Pipes",
  "pipesTagline": "Connect every pipe to the source",
  "pipesRules": "• Rotate the tiles so that every pipe connects back to the source (the ringed tile).\n• No pipe end may be left open, and the network may not contain loops.\n• Water flows through everything already connected to the source.\n• Tiles with a dot in the corner are fixed and already in place.\n\nTap a tile to rotate it clockwise; long-press / right-click rotates it back.",
  "famPlumbing": "Plumbing",
  "famPlumbingAbout": "Turn the pipe tiles until the water flows where it should",
  "channelName": "Channel",
  "channelTagline": "Lead the water from the source to the drain",
  "channelRules": "• Turn the tiles so that one pipe carries the water from the source (the ringed tile) to the drain (the tile with the square).\n• The other tiles don't matter, and pipe ends may stay open.\n• Water leaves a tile by all its other ends, but it goes straight across a bridge (a crossing), so the pipe can pass a bridge twice.\n• Tiles with a dot in the corner are fixed and already in place.\n\nTap a tile to rotate it clockwise; long-press / right-click rotates it back.",

  "shikakuName": "Shikaku",
  "shikakuTagline": "Split the grid into numbered rectangles",
  "shikakuRules": "• Divide the whole grid into rectangles (squares count too).\n• Every rectangle contains exactly one number.\n• That number equals the rectangle's area in cells.\n\nDrag from one corner to the opposite corner to draw a rectangle. Tap a rectangle to remove it.",

  "trailName": "Trail",
  "trailTagline": "One path through every cell, numbers in order",
  "trailRules": "• Draw a single path that starts at 1 and visits every cell exactly once.\n• The path moves up, down, left or right (no diagonals).\n• It must pass the numbers in order (1 → 2 → 3 → …) and finish on the last number.\n\nDrag to draw. Drag back over the path to undo steps, or tap a cell of the path to cut it there.",

  "atomsName": "Atoms",
  "atomsTagline": "Bond every atom to match its number",
  "atomsRules": "• Connect the atoms with horizontal or vertical bonds.\n• Each atom needs exactly as many bonds as its number.\n• Two atoms can share one or two bonds.\n• Bonds can't cross each other or pass through atoms.\n• All atoms must end up connected into one molecule.\n\nDrag from an atom towards a neighbour to add a bond (1 → 2 → none). You can also tap the space between two atoms.",

  "litsName": "Tetra",
  "litsTagline": "One tetromino in every region",
  "litsRules": "• Shade exactly 4 connected cells in every outlined region, forming an L, I, T or S shape (rotations and mirror images allowed).\n• All shaded cells together form one connected area.\n• No 2×2 block may be fully shaded.\n• Two identical shapes may not touch each other across a region border.\n\nTap a cell to cycle empty → shaded → dot (your \"not shaded\" note).",
  "labyrinthName": "Labyrinth",
  "labyrinthTagline": "Find the way from corner to corner",
  "labyrinthRules": "• Find the way through the maze from the entrance in the top-left corner to the exit in the bottom-right corner.\n• You can't pass through walls.\n\nDrag from the end of your path to walk on. Drag back to retrace your steps, or tap a cell of the path to go back there.",
  "tagDrive": "drive, car, taxi, pick up, deliver, pets, animals, fuel, route planning",
  "homewardName": "Homeward",
  "homewardTagline": "Drive every pet home before the fuel runs out",
  "homewardRules": "• Drive the car along the roads. Every road between two crossings takes one unit of fuel.\n• Drive to a pet to pick it up, then drive to the home with the same symbol to drop it off. Both happen by themselves.\n• The car has only so many seats: a pet you reach while every seat is taken keeps waiting. With a seat free, it always gets in.\n• Bring every pet home before the fuel runs out.\n\nTap a crossing next to the car, or one further along a straight road, to drive there. Or drag along the roads. If the fuel runs out, undo or restart.",
  "homewardFuel": "Fuel {left} / {limit}",
  "@homewardFuel": {"placeholders": {"left": {"type": "int"}, "limit": {"type": "int"}}},
  "homewardOutOfFuel": "Out of fuel: undo or restart",
  "tutHomeward1": "Tap a crossing next to the car to drive there. Every road costs one unit of fuel. Drive to the pet to pick it up, then bring it to the home with the same symbol.",
  "tutHomeward2": "This car has one seat. A pet you reach while the seat is taken keeps waiting, so take one pet home before you fetch the next. Bring both home.",
  "tutHomeward3": "Now there's no fuel to spare, and the car has two seats. Rushing to the nearest pet isn't always best: plan a drive that picks up both pets and then passes both homes.",
  "tutHomeward4": "Now a real board. Before you set off, look where each pet lives, and plan a drive that passes pets and homes on the way.",
  "tutHomewardS1": "Chain the trips: plan so that each drop-off lies on the way to the next pet, and pick up a pet early when its home lies along a road you will drive anyway. This board has no fuel to spare.",
  "tutHomewardS2": "Hard boards leave no fuel to spare and give the car only two seats. Plan the whole drive before the first road: a seat taken too early makes you drive past a pet you need, so sometimes take another road around a pet.",
  "blockadeName": "Blockade",
  "blockadeTagline": "Slip past the patrol ships into the harbour",
  "blockadeRules": "• Sail the boat to the harbour. Each turn the boat moves one cell (up, down, left or right) or waits where it is. Rocks block the way.\n• After every turn, each patrol ship sails one cell along its beat: back and forth along a line, or round and round a loop. Its beat is drawn on the board, and a ring shows where the ship will be after your next turn.\n• Don't get caught: never end a turn on a ship's cell, and never swap places with a ship. No ship ever enters the start or the harbour.\n• Reach the harbour within the turns you have.\n\nTap a cell next to the boat to sail there, or drag. Tap the boat or Wait to stay put for a turn. On a keyboard, use the arrow keys and the space bar. If you're caught or out of turns, undo or restart.",
  "blockadeTurn": "Turn {turn} / {limit}",
  "@blockadeTurn": {"placeholders": {"turn": {"type": "int"}, "limit": {"type": "int"}}},
  "blockadeWait": "Wait",
  "blockadeCaught": "Caught by a patrol ship: undo or restart",
  "blockadeOutOfTurns": "Out of turns: undo or restart",
  "tutBlockade1": "Tap a cell next to the boat to sail there, one cell per turn. Rocks block the way. Sail to the harbour.",
  "tutBlockade2": "After each of your turns, every patrol ship sails one cell along its beat, and the ring shows where it goes next. Don't end a turn where a ship will be, and don't swap places with one. Tap the boat or Wait to stay put for a turn.",
  "tutBlockade3": "This ship sails round and round the island, and there are no turns to spare: the counter shows how many you have. The short way runs head-on into the ship, and there's no time to wait. Go round the island the other way, right behind the ship.",
  "tutBlockade4": "Now a real board. Before you set off, watch where each ship goes and plan when to pass each one.",
  "tutBlockadeS1": "A ship never surprises you: its dots show its whole beat, and the ring shows where it will be after your turn. You may sail into a cell a ship is just leaving, as long as it isn't coming to the cell you leave. Here there are no turns to spare: count the ships' beats and slip through the gaps.",
  "tutBlockadeS2": "Hard boards leave no turns to spare. Plan the whole passage before the first turn: if you must wait, wait where no ship comes, and a detour right behind a ship often beats waiting for it.",
  "campName": "Campsite",
  "campTagline": "Pitch a tent next to every tree",
  "campRules": "• Pitch one tent for every tree, right next to it (up, down, left or right).\n• Every tree gets its own tent, and every tent belongs to one tree next to it.\n• Tents never touch each other, not even diagonally.\n• The numbers outside the grid tell how many tents are in each row and column.\n\nTap a cell to cycle empty → grass (your \"no tent here\" note) → tent. Right-click cycles back. Slide across cells to mark them all in one go. A long press puts grass on every cell where the placed tents leave no room for another.",
  "islandsName": "Islands",
  "islandsTagline": "Flood the sea around the numbered islands",
  "islandsRules": "• Shade the sea so that the unshaded cells form islands.\n• Every island contains exactly one number, which equals its size in cells.\n• Islands only touch the sea, never each other (diagonal corners are fine).\n• The whole sea is connected, and it has no 2×2 pools.\n\nTap a cell to cycle empty → sea → dot (your \"land\" note). Long-press / right-click cycles back.",
  "minesName": "Mines",
  "minesTagline": "Find every mine, logic only",
  "minesRules": "• Open every cell that has no mine.\n• A number tells how many mines are in the 8 cells around it.\n• You never need to guess: every board can be cleared by logic.\n• Opening a cell with no mines around it opens its neighbours too.\n\nTap a cell to dig, long-press / right-click to flag it (or switch to Flag below). Tap a number whose flags are all placed to dig the rest around it. Digging a mine flags it with a bang, and the game goes on.",
  "minesDig": "Dig",
  "minesFlag": "Flag",
  "minesBoom": "Boom! That was a mine, so it's flagged now",
  "lampsName": "Lamps",
  "lampsTagline": "Light up every cell, lamps never face each other",
  "lampsRules": "• Place lamps in the empty cells (not on walls). A lamp lights its own cell and its row and column until a wall.\n• Every empty cell must be lit.\n• No lamp may shine on another lamp.\n• A number on a wall tells how many lamps are right next to it (up, down, left or right).\n\nTap a cell to cycle empty → dot (your \"no lamp\" note) → lamp. Long-press / right-click cycles back.",
  "fenceName": "Fence",
  "fenceTagline": "One loop that fits around the numbers",
  "fenceRules": "• Draw one closed loop along the dotted lines.\n• The loop never crosses or touches itself.\n• A number tells how many of its cell's four sides the loop uses. Cells without a number can have any count.\n\nTap between two dots to draw a line, tap again to mark it with a cross, and once more to clear it. Drag from dot to dot to draw several lines, or to erase them if you start on a line.",
  "pearlsName": "Pearls",
  "pearlsTagline": "Thread one loop through all the pearls",
  "pearlsRules": "• Draw one closed loop through the centers of the cells. It never crosses or touches itself, and it doesn't have to visit every cell.\n• The loop passes through every pearl.\n• At a black pearl it turns, and it goes straight on through the cells before and after.\n• At a white pearl it goes straight, and it turns in the cell before or after (or both).\n\nDrag through cells to draw the loop, or drag along it to erase. Tap between two cells to cycle line → cross → empty.",
  "railsName": "Rails",
  "railsTagline": "Lay one track from the entry to the exit",
  "railsRules": "• Lay one track through the centers of the cells, from the entry on the left edge to the exit on the bottom edge.\n• The track never branches, crosses itself or closes into a loop, and it doesn't have to visit every cell.\n• The numbers above and to the right of the grid tell how many cells of each column and row the track passes through.\n• Pieces already on the board are fixed: the track runs through them exactly as shown.\n\nDrag through cells to lay track, or drag along it to erase. Tap the center of a cell to cycle empty → track note → dot (your \"no track here\" note), or tap between two cells to cycle track → cross → empty.",
  "blocksName": "Blocks",
  "blocksTagline": "Numbers 1 to k in every region of k cells",
  "blocksRules": "• Fill every cell with a number.\n• A region of k cells holds each number from 1 to k exactly once (a region of one cell holds a 1).\n• Equal numbers never touch, not even diagonally.\n• Given numbers are fixed.\n\nPick a number in the palette and tap cells to place it, or tap a cell first and then a number. The pencil button toggles small notes. Long-press / right-click clears a cell.",
  "pairsName": "Pairs",
  "pairsTagline": "Two shaded cells in every region, side by side",
  "pairsRules": "• Shade exactly two cells in every outlined region.\n• Every shaded cell touches exactly one other shaded cell side by side, so the shading is all pairs.\n• Pairs never touch each other side by side (corners are fine).\n\nTap a cell to cycle empty → shaded → dot (your \"not shaded\" note). Right-click cycles back. Slide across cells to mark them all in one go. A long press puts a dot on every cell the finished pairs rule out.",
  "plotsName": "Plots",
  "plotsTagline": "Split the grid into plots as big as their numbers",
  "plotsRules": "• Fill every cell with a number.\n• Equal numbers that touch side by side form a plot, and a plot has exactly as many cells as its number: a 3 lies in a plot of three cells.\n• Two plots of the same size never touch side by side (they would be one plot).\n• Some plots show no number at all.\n\nPick a number in the palette and tap cells to fill them, or tap a cell first and then a number. Lines appear between different numbers, so you can see the plots take shape.",
  "linksName": "Links",
  "linksTagline": "Join the pairs and fill the grid",
  "linksRules": "• Join each pair of equal dots with a path through neighbouring cells (no diagonals).\n• Paths never cross, branch or share a cell.\n• Together the paths fill every cell of the grid.\n\nDrag from a dot to draw its path; drawing across another path cuts it. Tap a dot to clear its path, or a cell of a path to cut the path there.",
  "arrowsName": "Arrows",
  "arrowsTagline": "Shade what the arrows count, loop through the rest",
  "arrowsRules": "• Shade some cells. Shaded cells never touch side by side.\n• Draw one closed loop through the centers of all the other cells. It never branches or crosses itself.\n• Clue cells (a number and an arrow) are neither shaded nor on the loop. A clue's number counts the shaded cells in its arrow's direction, all the way to the edge.\n\nDrag through cells to draw the loop, or along it to erase. Tap the center of a cell to cycle empty → shaded → dot (your \"on the loop\" note), or tap between two cells to cycle line → cross → empty.",

