  "tutorialStrategies": "Learn strategies",
  "strategiesTitle": "{name} strategies",
  "@strategiesTitle": {"placeholders":{"name":{"type":"String"}}},
  "strategiesFinishedBody": "You know the main techniques for solving {name}.",
  "@strategiesFinishedBody": {"placeholders":{"name":{"type":"String"}}},
  "learnBasicsTab": "Basics",
  "learnStrategiesTab": "Strategies",
  "learnStrategiesIntro": "For when you know the rules: each lesson shows a technique that strong players use, on a board that needs it.",

  "tutMambo1": "Fill every cell with a sun or a moon. Never three of the same in a row: after two suns side by side comes a moon. Tap a highlighted cell to cycle empty → sun → moon.",
  "tutMambo2": "Every row and column holds as many suns as moons. The top row already has its two suns, so its other cells are moons. The right column works the same way.",
  "tutMambo3": "An = between two cells means they hold the same symbol. Match the highlighted cells to their neighbours.",
  "tutMambo4": "A × means the two cells are different: one sun, one moon.",
  "tutMambo5": "Now a whole board: use every rule together. Tip: the palette button at the top lets you stamp one symbol on many cells, and a long press (or right-click) cycles backwards.",
  "tutMamboS1": "When no rule applies directly, ask “what if?”. The highlighted pair is joined by =, so both cells hold the same symbol. Two suns would give the top row three suns out of four, so both are moons.",
  "tutMamboS2": "A harder board, where you'll need that trick often: try a symbol in a cell and follow the rules a few steps. If something breaks, the other symbol is right. A × pair always holds one of each, so it counts as one sun and one moon in its row.",
  "tutSudoku1": "Each row, column and box (the thick outlines) holds every number from 1 to 4 once. This row is missing one number: pick it in the palette, then tap the empty cell.",
  "tutSudoku2": "By their row, these two cells could be 3 or 4. Their columns decide: each column is missing just one number.",
  "tutSudoku3": "The boxes count too: every box needs 1 to 4 once. Finish the last box.",
  "tutSudoku4": "Not sure yet? Take notes. Turn on the pencil next to the palette, select the highlighted cell and note every number it could still hold.",
  "tutSudoku5": "Now a whole puzzle. Selecting a cell tints its row, column and box, and highlights the same number elsewhere. Settings can remove notes for you when you place a number.",
  "tutSudokuS1": "Look at one number instead of one cell. The highlighted box needs a 1: the 1s in its columns and in its second row rule out every cell but one. Then finish the board the same way.",
  "tutSudokuS2": "Two more tricks. Pairs: two cells of a line or box that can only hold the same two numbers claim them. Pointing: if a box's spots for a number share a line, the rest of that line can't have it.",
  "tutSudokuS3": "Hidden pairs: when two numbers fit only in the same two cells of a row, column or box, those cells belong to them, so every other note there can go. The same works for three numbers in three cells.",
  "tutSudokuS4": "X-Wing: in two rows a number fits only in the same two columns. One row takes the left spot and the other the right one, so the rest of both columns can't have it. With three rows and columns it's a Swordfish.",
  "tutSudokuS5": "Y-Wing: a cell that holds x or y sees one cell of x or z and one of y or z. Whatever the first one is, one of the others is z, so z is ruled out wherever both of them see.",
  "tutSudokuS6": "Two links: when a number has only two spots in a row, one of them holds it. Take two such rows where one end of each shares a column: those two ends can't both hold it, so one of the other two ends does, and cells that see both of those can't. A row and a column whose ends share a box work the same way.",
  "tutSudokuS7": "W-Wing: two cells far apart that can only be x or y. If x has only two spots in some line or box, one seeing each cell, the two cells can't both be x. So one of them is y, and y is ruled out wherever both of them see.",
  "tutKings1": "Put exactly one crown in every row, every column and every colored region. Three are placed, and the last one has just one spot left. Tap it twice: first a dot, then a crown.",
  "tutKings2": "Crowns never touch, not even at the corners. Tap once to put a dot (your \"no crown here\" note) on each cell around this crown.",
  "tutKings3": "The crowns' rows and the touching rule leave just one cell of the highlighted region without a dot. Place its crown.",
  "tutKings4": "Now a whole board. Dot the cells you can rule out, and look for rows, columns or regions with a single free cell.",
  "tutKingsS1": "Look for a region that fits in one row or column. The highlighted region lies entirely in the bottom row, so that row's crown is in it: dot the other cells of the bottom row, then carry on.",
  "tutKingsS2": "Another trick: if a crown in a cell would rule out every cell of another region (by its row, its column or by touching), that cell can't hold a crown. Dot it. The same works for two regions squeezed into two rows.",
  "tutHues1": "Paint every blank cell. A number counts the blank cells around it (diagonals too) that end up in its own color. The blue 3 has exactly three blank neighbours, so all of them are blue. Pick a color in the palette and tap cells to paint them.",
  "tutHues2": "Numbers count down as you paint: they show how many matching cells are still missing. A 0 means no blank neighbour takes its color, and numbered cells never count. Start with the blue 3, then see what the pink 2 still needs.",
  "tutHues3": "Now a real board. Start with numbers that need all of their blank neighbours, or none of them.",
  "tutHuesS1": "Rule out colors. Each highlighted cell touches the blue 0, so it can't be blue, and the pink 0, so it can't be pink. Only yellow is left.",
  "tutHuesS2": "A harder board. Compare numbers that share blank neighbors: what one still needs may already be used up by the other. When stuck, try a color in a cell and see whether some number breaks.",
  "tutMosaic1": "The patch in the top-left corner is yours. Pick a color below: your patch takes it and swallows every touching cell of that color. Turn the whole board one color.",
  "tutMosaic2": "Mind the move limit: pick the color that grows your patch the most. Tapping a cell on the board also picks its color.",
  "tutMosaic3": "Now a real board, with a few moves to spare.",
  "tutMosaicS1": "Plan ahead. Reach the middle of the board early, since your patch then touches more colors, and whenever you can, pick a color that wipes that color off the board.",
  "tutBlend1": "The board is made of patches: touching cells of one color. Pick a color below, then tap a patch to repaint it. It merges with the touching patches of that color. Repaint the middle patch.",
  "tutBlend2": "One move can merge many patches. The middle patch touches four others: paint it to join them, then finish. You have only 2 moves.",
  "tutBlend3": "Now a real board. The chosen color stays selected, so you can paint several patches in a row.",
  "tutBlendS1": "Pick one patch in the middle and keep repainting it: each move then swallows every touching patch of the new color. Choose the color that most of its neighbors share.",
  "tutPop1": "Tap a group of two or more touching bubbles of one color to select it, then tap it again to pop it. Clear the board.",
  "tutPop2": "Bubbles above fall into the gaps, so new groups can form. Order matters: pop the highlighted group first.",
  "tutPop3": "When a column empties, the columns to its left slide right to close the gap. Pop the middle to bring the sides together.",
  "tutPop4": "A group of n bubbles scores n × (n − 1): 2 bubbles score 2, 5 score 20. Save up for a big group to reach 20 points.",
  "tutPop5": "Other modes: in Shifter every row also slides right to close its gaps, in Continuous new columns roll in from the left, and Mega does both. Goals: clear the board, reach a target score, or play freely for your best. The game ends when no group of 2 is left.",
  "tutPopS1": "Clearing a board takes planning. Before popping, ask what falls into the gap: pop groups that bring bubbles of one color together, and never leave a single bubble of a color on its own.",
  "tutPopS2": "Chasing points: a group of n scores n × (n − 1), so one group of 8 (56) beats four groups of 2 (8). Pop the other colors first to merge a color into one big group.",
  "tutMerge1": "Swipe (or press an arrow key) to slide every tile as far as it goes. Two equal tiles that meet merge into their sum. Make a 4.",
  "tutMerge2": "A tile merges only once per move: 4, 4, 8 slides into 8, 8, not 16. After every move a new 2 (sometimes a 4) appears. Build a 16.",
  "tutMerge3": "Keep your biggest tile in a corner and feed it step by step. Build a 32.",
  "tutMergeS1": "Build a chain: keep your tiles in order along one row with the biggest in the corner, like 64, 32, 16, 8. Then one new 8 at the end rolls all the way up. Make 128.",
  "tutSnake1": "The snake moves on its own: swipe (or press an arrow key) to turn it. It can't turn right back. Lead it to the apple.",
  "tutSnake2": "Every apple makes the snake one cell longer. Don't run into a wall or into your own body. Eat 3 apples.",
  "tutSnake3": "A real board: eat 5 apples. Tap the board to pause, and swipe to go on.",
  "tutSnakeS1": "A long snake needs room. Before you grab an apple, make sure you can get out again: the cells your tail leaves are always free, so following your tail is the safe way. Eat 3 apples.",
  "tutJunction1": "Trains come out of the tunnel and run on their own. Tap the switch to flip it: the lever points where the next train goes. Send each train to the station with its symbol. Tap the board to start.",
  "tutJunction2": "A train takes the branch a switch is set to as it passes, so set each switch before the train gets there, and flip it back for the next one if needed. Tap the board away from the switches to pause.",
  "tutJunction3": "A real board: get 6 trains to their stations. Watch the trains coming out of the tunnel and set the switches ahead of them.",
  "tutTurnabout1": "Swipe (or press an arrow key) to move your runner, the round piece, one cell. Keep the swipe going to move on. Walls block the way. Reach the star.",
  "tutTurnabout2": "Every few seconds the whole maze turns, and a round arrow warns you just before. Your swipes stay on screen: up is always up, so the way to the star may now lie in another direction. Reach the star.",
  "tutTurnabout3": "A race: the rival, the diamond, runs to the star the shortest way. Get there first! The round starts with your first move; tap the board to pause.",
  "tutPipes1": "Tap a tile to turn it clockwise (a long press or right-click turns it back). Connect every pipe to the source, the ringed tile. Water shows what's already connected.",
  "tutPipes2": "No pipe end may stay open, so no pipe can point off the board. Tiles with a dot in the corner are fixed and already right. Start at the edges and corners, where tiles have the fewest ways to turn.",
  "tutPipes3": "Now a real board. The network may not form loops.",
  "tutPipesS1": "Work inward from the rim. A straight on the edge must run along it, a corner holds only an elbow pointing inward, and a T on the edge turns its flat side to the edge. Every tile you settle limits its neighbors.",
  "tutPipesS2": "A hard board. The network may not form a loop: if turning a tile would close one, it must point elsewhere. And two dead ends never face each other, or they'd form a pair cut off from the rest.",
  "tutChannel1": "Tap a tile to turn it clockwise (a long press or right-click turns it back). Lead the water from the source, the ringed tile, to the drain, the tile with the square.",
  "tutChannel2": "Only the pipe from the source to the drain counts: other tiles may point anywhere, and ends may stay open. Water leaves a tile by all its other ends, so a T carries it on even if its third end leads nowhere.",
  "tutChannel3": "A crossing is a bridge: water goes straight across it and never turns there, so the pipe can pass it twice, once along and once across.",
  "tutChannel4": "Now a real board. Many tiles are there only to lead you astray.",
  "tutChannelS1": "Work from both ends. The drain is fixed too, so follow the pipe back from it as well: a straight must go on, a bend must turn, and the edge cuts off ways. Join the two stretches where they can meet.",
  "tutChannelS2": "A hard board, with bridges. When a way runs into a dead end, off the board or into tiles that can't take the water, go back to the last tile where you had a choice and try another way.",
  "tutShikaku1": "Split the grid into rectangles. Each holds exactly one number, equal to its area in cells. Drag from one corner to the opposite one to draw a rectangle.",
  "tutShikaku2": "A 1 is a rectangle on its own: just tap it. Tap a drawn rectangle to remove it. Here the 6 fits only one way.",
  "tutShikaku3": "Now a real board. Big numbers near the edges usually have the fewest ways to fit.",
  "tutShikakuS1": "Ask which numbers can reach a cell. The bottom-left corner is too far for the 4 and the 6 to cover with a rectangle of their size, so it belongs to the 2.",
  "tutShikakuS2": "A hard board. List the few rectangles a big number could use: cells that all of them cover belong to it, and a cell only one number can reach belongs to that number.",
  "tutTrail1": "Drag from 1 to draw one path through every cell, moving up, down, left or right. It ends on the last number.",
  "tutTrail2": "The path must pass the numbers in order: 1 → 2 → 3 → 4. Drag back over your path to undo steps, or tap a cell of it to cut it there.",
  "tutTrail3": "Now a real board. Cells in corners have only two ways in and out, so the path must use both.",
  "tutTrailS1": "Cells with only two free neighbors must be passed straight through: the path comes in one side and leaves by the other. Watch for cells your own path has just boxed in.",
  "tutTrailS2": "A hard board. Never cut the free cells into two parts: the path can't come back for the other part. And a cell with only one free neighbor is a dead end, allowed only for the last number.",
  "tutLabyrinth1": "Drag from the start in the top-left corner and walk to the flag in the bottom-right corner. Walls block the way.",
  "tutLabyrinth2": "A bigger maze. Hit a dead end? Drag back along your path, or tap any cell of it to return there. A quick drag follows straight corridors.",
  "tutLabyrinthS1": "Lost? Keep one hand on a wall: always take the rightmost opening. In a maze like this it always leads out, though not by the shortest way.",
  "tutLabyrinthS2": "Or work backwards: trace the way from the flag toward the start, and look for where the two routes meet. Dead ends near the flag are ruled out fast that way.",
  "tutAtoms1": "Connect the atoms with bonds. Each atom needs as many bonds as its number, and two atoms can share one or two. Drag from an atom toward a neighbour to add a bond (1 → 2 → none).",
  "tutAtoms2": "All atoms must join into one molecule, and bonds can't cross. Bonding the top-left 1 downwards would leave two separate pairs, so where does its bond go?",
  "tutAtoms3": "Now a real board. Start with atoms that have only one way to get their bonds.",
  "tutAtomsS1": "Compare an atom's number with its neighbors. The 4 in the corner has just two neighbors, and a pair can share at most two bonds, so both bonds are double. Likewise a 3 with two neighbors gets at least one bond to each.",
  "tutAtomsS2": "A hard board. Keep the molecule in one piece: two 1s never bond to each other, and two 2s never share a double bond, unless they're the only atoms. When stuck, try a bond and see if part of the board gets cut off.",
  "tutLits1": "Shade exactly 4 cells in every outlined region, forming an L, I, T or S. The top region has exactly 4 cells, so shade them all. Tap a cell to shade it.",
  "tutLits2": "No 2×2 block may be fully shaded, and two identical shapes may not touch across a border. Just one cell completes the left region. Which one?",
  "tutLits3": "Now a real board. All shaded cells must form one connected area. Tap twice for a dot, your note that a cell stays empty.",
  "tutLitsS1": "List the shapes each region can still hold. Cells that every possible shape covers are shaded, and cells that none covers stay empty. Small regions and regions squeezed by the 2×2 rule have the fewest options.",
  "tutLitsS2": "A harder board. When stuck, try one shape in a region: if it makes a 2×2 block, cuts the shaded area in two or puts two equal shapes side by side, it's wrong.",
  "tutCamp1": "Pitch one tent next to every tree: up, down, left or right, never diagonal. The numbers outside tell how many tents each row and column holds. Tap a cell twice: grass, then a tent.",
  "tutCamp2": "Tents never touch each other, not even diagonally. One tent is pitched already. Where can the other tree's tent go?",
  "tutCamp3": "Now a real board. A 0 means the whole row or column is grass, and every tree gets its own tent.",
  "tutCampS1": "Count the gaps. The top row needs 2 tents, and only its three highlighted cells can hold one. Two tents in three cells that may not touch must take both ends.",
  "tutCampS2": "A hard board, and some counts are hidden. When stuck, try a tent on a spot: if some tree is left with no place for its own tent, that spot is grass.",
  "tutIslands1": "Shade the sea so that the unshaded cells form islands. Each number is an island of exactly that many cells. Here the 1 is an island by itself: tap every other cell to make it sea.",
  "tutIslands2": "Islands never touch each other. A cell between two numbers must be sea, or it would join them into one island.",
  "tutIslands3": "The sea must stay connected and may never form a 2×2 pool. Grow the 3 so that neither happens. Tap twice for a dot, your note for land.",
  "tutIslands4": "Now a real board. Every island holds exactly one number.",
  "tutIslandsS1": "Find cells no island can reach. The 3 grows at most two steps from its number, the 2 one step and the 1 none. The highlighted cells are out of every island's reach, so they're sea.",
  "tutIslandsS2": "A hard board. Keep the sea in mind: it must stay connected, so a sea cell with one way out continues that way, and it may not form a 2×2 pool. When stuck, try a cell as land and see whether something breaks.",
  "tutLamps1": "Put lamps in the empty cells: tap twice (a dot, then a lamp). The dark cells are walls. A lamp lights its own row and column up to the walls. Light up every empty cell.",
  "tutLamps2": "A number on a wall tells how many lamps touch it (up, down, left or right). This 3 needs a lamp on each of its free sides.",
  "tutLamps3": "Lamps may never shine on each other, and a 0 means no lamp right next to it. Where does the second lamp go?",
  "tutLamps4": "Now a real board. Dots help you mark cells that can't hold a lamp.",
  "tutLampsS1": "Some cells have just one way to get light. The top-left corner can only be lit from itself or its two neighbors, and the 0 rules the neighbors out: the lamp goes in the corner. Then look at the 1.",
  "tutLampsS2": "A hard board. When stuck, try a lamp on a cell and follow what it forces: if some cell can no longer be lit, or a number can't be met, that cell gets a dot.",
  "tutFence1": "Draw one closed loop along the dotted lines. A number tells how many sides of its cell the loop uses. Tap between two dots to draw a line, or drag from dot to dot.",
  "tutFence2": "A 0 has no line around it. Tap a line again to turn it into a cross, your note that no line goes there. Cells without a number can have any count.",
  "tutFence3": "The loop never branches or crosses itself: every dot has either no line or two. Numbers next to the board's edge are a good place to start.",
  "tutFence4": "Now a real board. Start with the 0s and 3s.",
  "tutFenceS1": "Learn a few patterns. Two 3s side by side always have a line between them and a line on each far side: any other way leaves one of them short. The 0 above helps too.",
  "tutFenceS2": "Corners are strong. A 1 in a corner never uses its two outer sides: the loop would have to turn right there and use both. A 3 in a corner always uses both.",
  "tutFenceS3": "A hard board. When stuck, try a line on one edge and follow it: if it leads to a dead end, a number it can't satisfy, or a small loop that leaves others out, that edge gets a cross.",
  "tutPearls1": "Drag through the cells to draw one closed loop. At a black pearl the loop turns, then runs straight through the next cell on both sides.",
  "tutPearls2": "At a white pearl the loop goes straight through, and it turns in the cell just before or after it (or both).",
  "tutPearls3": "Now a real board. The loop doesn't have to visit every cell, and it never crosses or touches itself.",
  "tutPearlsS1": "A black pearl can't turn toward an edge that's too close: the loop needs two straight cells on each side. Both black pearls here are too close to two edges, so their directions are fixed.",
  "tutPearlsS2": "A hard board. Three white pearls in a row can't all lie on one straight stretch (the middle one needs a turn next to it), so the loop crosses them. When stuck, try a line and see if some pearl breaks.",
  "tutRails1": "Drag through the cells to lay one track from the entry on the left to the exit at the bottom. The numbers above and to the right count the track cells in each column and row.",
  "tutRails2": "Pieces already on the board are fixed: the track runs through them exactly as shown. A 0 means no track in that row or column at all.",
  "tutRails3": "Now a real board. The track never branches or crosses itself, and it doesn't have to visit every cell.",
  "tutRailsS1": "Start with lines whose count leaves no choice. The second row needs 4 track cells and has only 4, and so does the right column. Then join the ends.",
  "tutRailsS2": "A hard board. A count that's already used up blocks the rest of its line, and a track cell always needs exactly two track neighbors. When stuck, try a piece and check that the counts still fit.",
  "tutBlocks1": "Every region of k cells holds the numbers 1 to k once each. Each highlighted cell is the last gap in its region: pick the missing number in the palette, then tap the cell.",
  "tutBlocks2": "Equal numbers never touch, not even at the corners. The top-left region needs a 1 and a 2, and one of its cells already touches a 2. Fill the bottom row the same way.",
  "tutBlocks3": "Now a real board. Start with small regions and with cells whose neighbours rule out most numbers. Pencil notes help.",
  "tutBlocksS1": "Pointing: note where each region can still put a number. When all those cells touch the same outside cell, that cell can't hold the number, since it would touch it. Pencil notes help you see it.",
  "tutBlocksS2": "A hard board. When nothing else works, pick a cell with just two possible numbers and try one: if it soon leaves a region with no place for a number, the other one is right.",
  "tutPairs1": "Shade exactly two cells in every region, so that each shaded cell touches exactly one other: the shading comes in pairs. The highlighted region has just two cells, so shade both.",
  "tutPairs2": "Pairs never touch each other side by side. The top pair is finished, so the highlighted cells next to it stay unshaded: put a dot there (tap twice), then finish the board.",
  "tutPairs3": "Now a real board. Small regions and cells boxed in by dots are good places to start.",
  "tutPairsS1": "Try every way to finish a small region: a cell shaded in all of them is shaded, and a cell shaded in none gets a dot. An L of three cells, for example, always shades its corner.",
  "tutPairsS2": "A hard board. When stuck, shade a cell and follow the rules: if some region can no longer get its two cells, or two pairs would touch, that cell stays unshaded.",
  "tutPlots1": "Fill every cell with a number. Equal numbers that touch side by side form a plot with exactly that many cells. The highlighted 3 needs two more cells for its plot.",
  "tutPlots2": "Two plots of the same size can't touch: they would join into one plot that is too big. The highlighted cell touches two plots of 2, so it can't be a 2.",
  "tutPlots3": "Now a real board. Some plots show no number at all: work out their size from the room that's left.",
  "tutPlotsS1": "Look for pockets. The two highlighted cells are fenced in by finished plots, so they can only join each other. Two 1s may not touch, so together they are a plot of 2.",
  "tutPlotsS2": "A hard board. When nothing is certain, pick a cell with only two or three possible numbers and test each one: a number that leaves some plot unable to reach its size is out.",
  "tutLinks1": "Drag from a dot to its twin to join them. Paths go through neighbouring cells, never diagonally.",
  "tutLinks2": "Paths never cross, and together they fill every cell, so some have to take the long way round.",
  "tutLinks3": "Now a real board. Corners and edges leave the fewest ways to go, so start there.",
  "tutLinksS1": "Fill the tight spots first. An empty corner has only two neighbors, so the path through it uses both. The same goes for any cell squeezed down to two free neighbors.",
  "tutLinksS2": "A hard board. In a puzzle with one answer, a path never folds back beside itself (it could take a shortcut), so no 2×2 square belongs to a single path. And never leave an empty cell that no path can still reach.",
  "tutArrows1": "Draw one loop through the centers of all the empty cells: drag from cell to cell. The clue cell in the middle is never on the loop. Its 0 says no cell above it is shaded, so here nothing is.",
  "tutArrows2": "Now two cells must be shaded. Each clue counts the shaded cells in its arrow's direction: find them and tap their centers to shade them. Shaded cells never touch side by side. Then draw the loop through all the other cells.",
  "tutArrows3": "Now a real board. Cells next to a shaded cell are always on the loop, and a loop cell needs two ways out.",
  "tutArrowsS1": "Look for tight clues. The 2 in the middle row has just three cells to its right, and its two shaded cells may not touch, so they take the first and the last. The 2 in the top row is even easier: it has only two free cells.",
  "tutArrowsS2": "A hard board. Every cell that isn't shaded or a clue is on the loop, so a cell with only two free neighbors has its path fixed. When stuck, try shading a cell and see if a loop cell is left with fewer than two ways out.",
  "tutMines1": "A number counts the mines in the 8 cells around it. Each 1 here touches just one closed cell, so that cell is a mine. Flag it: long-press or right-click it, or switch to Flag below and tap it.",
  "tutMines2": "This 1 already has its mine flagged, so every other cell around it is safe. Dig them, or tap the 1 itself to dig them all at once.",
  "tutMines3": "A cell with no mines around it opens its neighbours for you. Dig the highlighted corner.",
  "tutMines4": "Now a real board. You never need to guess. If you dig a mine by mistake, it just gets flagged and the game goes on.",
  "tutMinesS1": "Compare neighboring numbers. The 2 sees three closed cells, the 1 on its left sees only the first two, so the third one is a mine. The same works from the right. Then the middle cell is safe.",
  "tutMinesS2": "A hard board. Keep comparing numbers that share closed cells. Near the end, count what's left: the mine counter can settle the last closed cells.",
