  "formulaName": "Formula",
  "formulaTagline": "Guess the hidden equation",
  "formulaRules": "• An equation is hidden. Find it in six guesses.\n• Every guess must be a true equation that fills the row: a calculation, then = and its value.\n• Each tile of a guess gets a mark. Filled: the right symbol in the right place. Outlined: in the equation, but somewhere else. Faded: not in the equation, or not that many times.\n• The usual order: brackets first, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −.\n\nEasy equations only add and subtract, medium ones multiply or divide too, hard ones have brackets or powers (3² = 3 × 3, 2³ = 2 × 2 × 2). The keys remember the marks. Tap a tile to type there. A guess can't be taken back; when the rows run out, restart to try again.",
  "formulaIncomplete": "Fill the whole row first",
  "formulaMalformed": "Not an equation: a calculation, then = and a number",
  "formulaUntrue": "The two sides aren't equal",
  "formulaOutOfGuesses": "Out of guesses: restart to try again",
  "formulaSolvedIn": "{count, plural, =1{Solved in one guess!} other{Solved in {count} guesses}}",
  "@formulaSolvedIn": {"placeholders": {"count": {"type": "int"}}},
  "tutFormula1": "An equation is hidden behind this board. Every guess is a true equation that fills a row. Type 1+4=5 (or any other) and press Submit.",
  "tutFormula2": "A guess marks its tiles. Filled: right symbol, right place. Outlined: in the equation, but elsewhere. Faded: not in it. Here = is in place, 4, 5 and 9 belong elsewhere and there is no +. Find the equation.",
  "tutFormula3": "The first 2 is in place, but the second one is faded: the equation has only one 2. The keys below remember every mark. Finish the board.",
  "tutFormula4": "Now a real board: six guesses for an equation of six tiles. It only adds or subtracts.",
  "tutFormulaS1": "Open with a guess that tests many different symbols: the more tiles get a mark, the more you learn. Enter an equation with at least seven different symbols, like 9×8−7=65.",
  "tutFormulaS2": "Hard boards add brackets and powers: 3² is 3 × 3, 2³ is 2 × 2 × 2. Brackets are worked out first, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −. Find the equation.",
  "tutFormulaS3": "The big one: ten tiles with a bracket or a power. Start by finding where = stands: it tells how long the result is.",
  "orbitsName": "Orbits",
  "orbitsTagline": "Give every star a region that turns around it",
  "orbitsRules": "• Split the whole grid into regions, one for each star.\n• A region is one connected piece with its star in the exact middle.\n• Half a turn around the star leaves the region looking the same: every cell has a twin on the opposite side of the star.\n\nDrag from a star, or from a cell of its region, over the cells to add; each one brings its twin along. Or tap a star to pick it, then tap the cells to give it, even ones away from its region. Tap a cell of the picked star (or any filled cell when no star is picked) to clear it and its twin.",
  "tutOrbits1": "Split the grid into regions, one for each star. The star sits in the exact middle of its region, so every cell has a twin on the opposite side. Drag from the star in the right column to the cell above it: the twin below is filled in too.",
  "tutOrbits2": "A star can sit inside a cell, on the line between two cells, or on a corner where four meet. The cells it touches are its own from the start. Give every remaining cell to a star: drag from the star, or tap the star and then the cells. Tap a cell of the picked star to clear it and its twin.",
  "tutOrbits3": "A region can have any shape, as long as it is one piece and looks the same after half a turn around its star. A cell can't join a star if its twin would fall off the board or onto another star's cell.",
  "tutOrbits4": "Now a real board. Start with cells that only one star can take: the corners and edges are good places to look.",
  "tutOrbitsS1": "Keep regions in one piece. When a cell belongs to a star but isn't joined to it yet, look at the ways to connect them: a cell that every way passes through belongs to the star too, and so does its twin.",
  "tutOrbitsS2": "A hard board. When nothing is certain, pick a cell that only two stars can take and try one: if some cell is then left with no star at all, the cell belongs to the other star.",
  "exOrbitsOnly": "{cell} belongs to the star at {star}: no other star can take it.",
  "@exOrbitsOnly": {"placeholders": {"cell": {}, "star": {}}},
  "exOrbitsTwin": "{cell} belongs to the star at {star}: it's the twin of {other}.",
