  "inlayName": "Inlay",
  "inlayTagline": "Fit every piece into the shape",
  "inlayRules": "• Fill the shape with all the pieces from the tray. Every cell of the shape gets covered, and pieces never overlap.\n• Any way that fits them all wins.\n• Pieces may be turned. On hard boards they may be flipped over too.\n\nDrag a piece onto the shape: it snaps to the cells. Drag a laid piece to move it, or off the shape to put it back in the tray. Tap a piece to turn it a quarter. On hard boards, Flip mirrors the piece you touched last (on a computer, right-click a piece).",
  "inlayFlip": "Flip",
  "inlayNoRoom": "No room to turn it here",
  "tutInlay1": "Drag each piece from the tray onto the shape. It snaps to the cells. Cover the whole shape with the pieces.",
  "tutInlay2": "Tap a piece to turn it a quarter, in the tray or on the board. Turn these pieces so they fit.",
  "tutInlay3": "The bar on the board leaves no room for the other pieces. Drag a laid piece to move it, or drag it off the shape to put it back in the tray.",
  "tutInlay4": "On hard boards a piece can also be flipped over. Touch a piece, then tap Flip (on a computer, right-click it). One piece here only fits flipped.",
  "tutInlay5": "Now a real board. Any way that fits all the pieces wins.",
  "tutInlayS1": "Start where the pieces have the fewest ways in: the corners, narrow arms and small pockets. The cell at the tip of the arm has a single neighbour: find the piece that fits there, and lay it first.",
  "tutInlayS2": "Lay the biggest and most awkward pieces first, while there is still room for them; small pieces fill the last gaps. Watch for pockets: a gap smaller than every piece left means a piece must move. On hard boards some pieces only fit flipped.",
  "skylineName": "Skyline",
  "skylineTagline": "How many towers can you see from each side?",
  "skylineRules": "• Put a tower in every cell, 1 to N floors tall (N = grid size).\n• Every row and column holds each height exactly once.\n• A number outside the grid tells how many towers you can see from there, looking along its row or column: a tower hides every shorter one behind it.\n\nPick a height in the palette and tap cells to place it, or tap a cell first and then a height. The pencil button toggles small notes. Long-press / right-click clears a cell.",
  "tutSkyline1": "Build a skyline: every row and column holds one tower of each height from 1 to 4. One is missing here: pick its height in the palette, then tap the empty cell.",
  "tutSkyline2": "A clue counts the towers you can see from its side: each tower hides the shorter ones behind it. The clue left of the top row says 2. Fill the empty cells so that only two towers show from there.",
  "tutSkyline3": "A 1 means the tallest tower stands right at the clue and hides all the rest. A 4 means every tower is in sight, so they climb 1, 2, 3, 4. Fill the row.",
  "tutSkyline4": "Now a whole board. Note the heights a cell can still hold with the pencil, and let the clues narrow them down.",
  "tutSkylineS1": "The tallest tower hides everything behind it, so a 3 keeps it out of the first two cells. In general, a big clue needs short towers near it: next to a 3 on a board of 5, at most 3 floors.",
  "tutSkylineS2": "Stuck? Take a row or column with clues and go through every way to fill it that fits your notes and its clues. A height that never lands in a cell can be crossed off there.",
  "tutSkylineS3": "The hardest boards need a guess you can disprove. Suppose a cell holds one of its last two or three heights and follow on; if that runs into a contradiction, cross the height off.",
