  "famLatin": "Latin squares",
  "famLatinAbout": "Every row and column holds each number once, and the clues tell you more",
  "cagesName": "Cages",
  "cagesTagline": "Add, take away, multiply and divide to fill each cage",
  "cagesRules": "• Fill the grid with the numbers 1 to N (N = grid size), each once in every row and column.\n• Thick outlines split the grid into cages. The numbers in a cage make the clue in its corner with the sign shown: added up (+), multiplied (×), or, in a cage of two cells, the bigger minus the smaller (−) or the bigger divided by the smaller (÷). A cage of one cell simply holds its number.\n• A number may repeat in a cage, as long as not in the same row or column.\n\nPick a number in the palette and tap cells to place it, or tap a cell first and then a number. The pencil button toggles small notes. Long-press / right-click clears a cell.",
  "tutCages1": "Every row and column holds 1 to 4 once. The outlines group cells into cages, and the numbers in a cage make its clue: in a 7+ cage they add up to 7. Fill the empty cells.",
  "tutCages2": "In a cage of two cells, − is the bigger number minus the smaller, and ÷ the bigger divided by the smaller; either cell can hold the bigger one. Fill the top two rows.",
  "tutCages3": "× multiplies. A number may repeat in a cage as long as it's not twice in a row or column, like 1 × 2 × 1 for a 2×. Rows and columns alone allow two ways here: let the cages pick one.",
  "tutCages4": "Now a whole board. Start with the cages that can be made in few ways, note what a cell can still hold with the pencil, and let rows and columns narrow it down.",
  "tutCagesS1": "Go through the ways to make a cage. Two cells making 3+ can only be 1 and 2; a 4− on a board of 5 is 1 and 5. A number that no way puts into a cell can be crossed off there.",
  "tutCagesS2": "If every way to fill a cage puts some number into its cells of one row, no other cell of that row can have it. A 2÷ in a row on a board of 5 is 1 and 2 or 2 and 4: a 2 either way. And when two cells of a row can only hold the same two numbers, those two are theirs.",
  "tutCagesS3": "The hardest boards need a guess you can disprove. Suppose a cell holds one of its last two or three numbers and follow on; if that runs into a contradiction, cross the number off.",
  "exCagesSingle": "{cell} must be {value}: its cage is that one cell.",
  "@exCagesSingle": {"placeholders": {"cell": {}, "value": {}}},
  "exCagesCombo": "No way to fill the {clue} cage at {cage} puts {values} into {cell}.",
