  "signsName": "Signs",
  "signsTagline": "Follow the signs: which number is bigger?",
  "signsRules": "• Fill the grid with the numbers 1 to N (N = grid size), each once in every row and column.\n• A sign between two neighbouring cells points at the smaller number, like 2 < 3: its open side faces the bigger one. Signs work along rows and down columns.\n• A few numbers may be given.\n\nPick a number in the palette and tap cells to place it, or tap a cell first and then a number. The pencil button toggles small notes. Long-press / right-click clears a cell.",
  "tutSigns1": "Every row and column holds 1 to 4 once. A sign between two cells points at the smaller number, like 1 < 2, and works up and down too. Rows and columns alone allow two ways here: let the signs pick one.",
  "tutSigns2": "Signs can form a chain: every cell of the third row is bigger than the next. The first one has three smaller cells after it, so it must be 4, and the last must be 1. Fill the bottom two rows.",
  "tutSigns3": "The second cell of the second row is bigger than both its neighbours. They need two different smaller numbers, so it can't be 1 or 2. Fill the top two rows.",
  "tutSigns4": "Now a whole board. Start where signs bunch up or a number is given, note what a cell can still hold with the pencil, and let rows and columns narrow it down.",
  "tutSignsS1": "Follow the signs. A cell smaller than its neighbour can't reach the biggest number the neighbour can still hold, and a bigger one can't go down to its smallest. Along a chain a < b < c the first is at most N − 2. Cross off what the signs rule out.",
  "tutSignsS2": "A cell bigger than both its neighbours in a row needs two different numbers below it, so it is at least 3; a run of signs counts the same way. And when two cells of a row can only hold the same two numbers, those two are theirs.",
  "tutSignsS3": "The hardest boards need a guess you can disprove. Suppose a cell holds one of its last two or three numbers and follow on; if that runs into a contradiction, cross the number off.",
  "exSignsLess": "{cell} is smaller than {other}, which is at most {limit}, so {cell} can't be {values}.",
  "@exSignsLess": {"placeholders": {"cell": {}, "other": {}, "limit": {}, "values": {}}},
  "exSignsMore": "{cell} is bigger than {other}, which is at least {limit}, so {cell} can't be {values}.",
