  "soloName": "Solo",
  "soloTagline": "Shade the repeats until every number stands alone",
  "soloRules": "• Every cell holds a number. Shade some of them so that no number shows twice in a row or column.\n• Shaded cells never touch side by side.\n• The cells left form one connected area.\n• Only shade a number that still shows, unshaded, somewhere in its row or column.\n\nTap a cell to cycle: shaded → circled (your \"this one stays\" note) → empty. Long-press / right-click cycles back. Circles are optional: the puzzle is done once the right numbers are shaded.",
  "tutSolo1": "Shade numbers so that none shows twice in a row or column. Shaded cells never touch side by side, and the cells left stay in one piece. Only shade a number that repeats. Tap a cell to shade it.",
  "tutSolo2": "Shaded cells never touch, so the numbers next to a shaded cell stay. The top-left 1 is shaded: tap the highlighted numbers twice to circle them (your note that they stay), then finish the board.",
  "tutSolo3": "Two equal numbers with one cell between them: one of them is shaded, and either one would touch the middle cell, so the middle number stays. Circle the highlighted 1, then finish the board.",
  "tutSolo4": "The cells left must form one piece. Once the 1 next to the top-left 1 is shaded, the 2 below it is that corner's only way out, so it stays. Finish the board.",
  "tutSolo5": "Now a real board. Start with numbers that show only once in their row and column, and with equal numbers close together.",
  "tutSoloS1": "Two equal numbers side by side can't both be shaded, so one of them stays, and every other copy of that number in their row or column is shaded. The top row has two 4s side by side: shade the highlighted 4.",
  "tutSoloS2": "Keep the cells left in one piece: if shading a cell would cut them in two, with numbers that stay on both sides, that cell stays. Watch for shaded cells lining up diagonally toward an edge.",
  "tutSoloS3": "A hard board. When stuck, shade a number and follow on: if two equal numbers end up left in one line, or some cells get cut off, that number stays.",
