  "dominoName": "Dominoes",
  "dominoTagline": "Find where every domino of the set lies",
  "dominoRules": "• The grid is a full set of dominoes with the outlines rubbed off: every pair of numbers, from 0–0 up to the highest double, is on exactly one domino.\n• Split the grid into dominoes of two neighbouring cells, so that every pair shows up once.\n\nTap the line between two cells to join them into a domino; tap again for a cross (your \"no domino here\" note) and once more to clear it. Drag across cells to lay dominoes along the way. A new domino takes its cells away from the dominoes that had them.",
  "tutDomino1": "The grid is a set of dominoes with the outlines rubbed off, and every pair of numbers is on exactly one domino. This small set has 0–0, 0–1 and 1–1. Join two neighbouring cells by tapping the line between them, or drag from one cell to the other.",
  "tutDomino2": "A pair of numbers that sits side by side in only one place must be a domino there. The highlighted 0 and 0 are the only two 0s next to each other: join them, then finish the board.",
  "tutDomino3": "Every cell is in a domino. The 2–2 is placed, so the highlighted 0 has only one neighbour left to pair with. Join them, then finish the board.",
  "tutDomino4": "Every pair is used once. The 2–3 domino is placed already, so the two other 2–3s in the top corner can't be dominoes: tap the line between each of them twice to cross it out. Then see which neighbour the corner 2 has left, and finish the board.",
  "tutDomino5": "Now a real board. Start with pairs that fit in one place only, and with cells that have one neighbour left. Cross out the places of every pair you have placed.",
  "tutDominoS1": "When every place left for a pair covers the same cell, that cell belongs to that pair, so it can't pair with any other neighbour. Every place left for the 3–3 covers the highlighted cell: cross out its other neighbours, then finish the board.",
  "tutDominoS2": "When every domino a cell can be in is the same pair, that pair lies at this cell, so cross out its places elsewhere. The highlighted 3 can only be in a 1–3, either way: cross out the other 1–3s.",
  "tutDominoS3": "A hard board. Watch for open cells walled into a group of an odd size: dominoes can't fill it. When stuck, try a domino and follow on: if a cell or a pair ends up with no place, or an odd group appears, that domino is wrong.",
  "exDominoOnlySpot": "{a}–{b} fits only at {link}, so that is a domino.",
  "@exDominoOnlySpot": {"placeholders": {"a": {}, "b": {}, "link": {}}},
