  "tagShips": "ships, boats, fleet, sea, navy, hidden",
  "fleetName": "Fleet",
  "fleetTagline": "Find the hidden ships from the counts at the edge",
  "fleetRules": "• A fleet of ships hides in the grid; the ships it has are shown below the grid. Each ship is a straight line of cells, across or down.\n• Ships never touch each other, not even diagonally.\n• The numbers outside the grid tell how many ship cells each row and column holds.\n• Some cells are shown from the start: water, or a piece of a ship. A rounded end shows where its ship ends, a square piece sits in the middle of a ship, and a round piece is a whole ship of one cell.\n\nTap a cell to cycle: ship → water (your \"no ship here\" note) → empty. Right-click cycles back. Slide across cells to mark them all in one go. A long press puts water on every cell where the placed ships leave no room for another. Water is optional: the puzzle is done once every ship is in place. Ships you finish fade in the list below the grid once water or the edge closes them in.",
  "tutFleet1": "A fleet of ships hides in the grid, and the list below it shows its ships. The numbers outside tell how many ship cells each row and column holds. Here the fleet is three ships of one cell. Tap a cell to put a ship there, tap again for water.",
  "tutFleet2": "Ships are straight lines of cells, and they never touch, not even diagonally. The bottom row needs two ship cells, but this fleet has no ship of two cells, so they are two separate ships with a gap between them. Finish the board.",
  "tutFleet3": "Some pieces are shown from the start. A rounded end means its ship goes on the other way: the piece in the corner is rounded at the top, so the cell below it is a ship too. Finish the board.",
  "tutFleet4": "Now a real board. Start with rows and columns whose count is zero or full, put water around the ships you know, and keep an eye on which ships are still missing.",
  "tutFleetS1": "Look for where the missing ships still fit. A cell no missing ship can pass through is water, and when the longest missing ship has only a few places left, the cells they all share are ship. Count the places in the rows and columns with room for it.",
  "tutFleetS2": "A hard board: some counts are hidden. When stuck, suppose a cell is a ship and follow on: if a row overflows, ships touch or a ship has nowhere to go, that cell is water.",
