  "lookoutName": "Lookout",
  "lookoutTagline": "Shade cells so each number sees exactly as many as it says",
  "lookoutRules": "• Each number tells how many cells it sees across and down, itself included: along its row and column, out to the first shaded cell or the edge.\n• Numbered cells are never shaded.\n• Shaded cells never touch side by side.\n• The unshaded cells form one connected area.\n\nTap a cell to cycle: shaded → dot (your \"this one stays\" note) → empty. Right-click cycles back. A long press dots the cells next to shaded ones and every cell a number sees once shaded cells close its view at its count. Dots are optional: the puzzle is done once the right cells are shaded.",
  "tutLookout1": "Each number counts the cells it sees along its row and column, itself included, up to a shaded cell or the edge. Shaded cells never touch side by side, and numbered cells are never shaded. The bottom 2 already sees itself and the 4 above it, so its view must stop everywhere else: shade the cells beside it and the one beyond the 4.",
  "tutLookout2": "Count how far a number could see at most. The 5 could see five cells at most: itself, the two beside it and the two above it. So it sees all of them, and they all stay. Finish the board.",
  "tutLookout3": "The unshaded cells must stay in one piece. Once the cell under the 3 is shaded, the bottom-right corner can only reach the others through the cell to its left, so that cell stays. Tap a cell twice to dot it (your note that it stays), then finish the board.",
  "tutLookout4": "Now a real board. Start with numbers that already see all they may in some direction, and big numbers that can barely see far enough.",
  "tutLookoutS1": "A number's view ends at the edge or at a shaded cell, never at another number. The 2 sees one cell besides itself. To the left its view can't stop after one cell, because the next one is the 3, so it sees the cell below it instead: the cell to its left and the second cell down are shaded.",
  "tutLookoutS2": "Try every way to split a number's count among its four directions, each view ending at the edge or at a cell that can be shaded. A cell every way sees stays, a cell where every way stops is shaded. Also, a cell whose shading would cut the unshaded cells in two stays.",
  "tutLookoutS3": "A hard board. When stuck, shade a cell and follow on: if a number then sees too many or too few cells, two shaded cells touch, or some cells get cut off, that cell stays.",
  "tagSight": "sight, see, view, visible, look, line of sight, watch",
  "exLookoutReach": "{cell} stays: the {count} at {clue} can't see enough cells in the other directions, so it sees at least this far.",
  "@exLookoutReach": {"placeholders": {"cell": {}, "clue": {}, "count": {"type": "int"}}},
