  "waymarksName": "Waymarks",
  "waymarksTagline": "Each sign points the way to the next number",
  "waymarksRules": "• Every cell has an arrow, except the last one, which has a ring.\n• Number the cells 1, 2, 3, … up to the last, so that each arrow points at the cell with the next number. That cell can be any distance away along the arrow's line, with other cells in between.\n• Some numbers are shown, always 1 and the last.\n\nDrag from a cell to the cell it leads on to, or tap one and then the other, to link them. Linked cells count on from a shown number in their chain; a chain without one gets a letter (a, a+1, …) until it joins one. Tap a picked cell again to undo its link, or long-press (right-click) a cell to cut all its links.",
  "tutWaymarks1": "Number the cells from 1 to the ring: each arrow points at the next number, any distance along its line. An arrow with only one free cell in its way leads there. Drag from a cell to the next one to link them, or tap one and then the other.",
  "tutWaymarks2": "Look backwards too: every cell except 1 needs a cell before it. If only one free arrow points at a cell, that cell comes right after the arrow's cell.",
  "tutWaymarks3": "Linked cells count on from a shown number, and a chain without one gets a letter: a, a+1, … Two shown numbers one apart, like 12 and 13, are always linked, and cells whose numbers aren't one apart never are.",
  "tutWaymarks4": "A chain never leads back to its own first cell: that would close a loop, and the numbers only go up. So the end of a chain can't link to the start of the same chain.",
  "tutWaymarks5": "Now a real board. Link the cells with only one way on or one way in, count on from the shown numbers, and keep every chain from looping back to its start.",
  "tutWaymarksS1": "Think about which numbers a cell can still be. Its number is one more than a number of a cell that can lead to it, and one less than a number of a cell it can lead to, and no number is used twice. Narrow the cells down this way until a link is left with only one choice.",
  "tutWaymarksS2": "Two cells can only be linked if their numbers can be one apart. When no number the second cell can be is one more than a number the first can be, cross that link off, and look for what is left.",
  "tutWaymarksS3": "A hard board. When stuck, suppose a link and follow on: if a cell is left with no way on, no way in or no number, that link is wrong.",
  "exWaymarksOnlyOut": "{from} can only lead on to {to}: its arrow has no other cell left.",
  "@exWaymarksOnlyOut": {"placeholders": {"from": {}, "to": {}}},
  "exWaymarksOnlyIn": "Only {from} can still lead to {to}, so {to} comes right after it.",
