  "mercuryName": "Mercury",
  "mercuryTagline": "Fill the tubes from the bulb up to match the counts",
  "mercuryRules": "• The grid is covered with tubes, straight or bent, each starting at a round bulb.\n• Fill some cells with mercury. In every tube it starts at the bulb and goes on without gaps, as far as you like: a tube may stay empty, fill part of the way or fill up to its tip.\n• The numbers outside the grid tell how many filled cells each row and column holds. On harder boards some are hidden.\n\nTap a cell to cycle: mercury → empty (your note) → unmarked. Right-click cycles back. Slide across cells to fill them all in one go. A long press fills every cell between your mercury and its bulb, and marks empty the cells past your empty marks and in rows and columns that have all their mercury. Empty marks are optional: the puzzle is done once all the mercury is in place.",
  "famFilling": "Filling",
  "famFillingAbout": "Fill each container from one end up to a level so that the counts outside come out",
  "tagTemperature": "temperature, heat, fever, degrees, gauge, tube, bulb, mercury, fill, level",
  "tutMercury1": "The grid is covered with tubes, and mercury fills each one from its round bulb. The numbers outside tell how many filled cells each row and column holds. Tap a cell to fill it, tap again to mark it empty.",
  "tutMercury2": "Mercury always starts at the bulb and leaves no gaps: when a cell is filled, so is every cell between it and the bulb, even round a bend. And a cell left empty keeps the rest of its tube empty, up to the tip. Finish the board.",
  "tutMercury3": "Think ahead: filling a cell fills everything back to its bulb. When that would put more filled cells into a row or column than its number allows, the cell stays empty. Leaving a cell empty empties its tube up to the tip, so when that would leave a line short, the cell is filled. Finish the board.",
  "tutMercury4": "Now a real board. Start with rows and columns that are empty or full, fill back to the bulbs, and before you fill a cell, count how much mercury it brings along.",
  "tutMercuryS1": "Two lines that share several tubes are worth counting together: each shared tube fills them in a set order from its bulb, so not every split of mercury between them is possible. Here columns D and E share three tubes. Try the ways those tubes can make both numbers at once: a cell that comes out the same every time is settled.",
  "tutMercuryS2": "A medium board: some counts are hidden, and some steps need two lines counted together. Look for rows and columns that share tubes, such as a tube that bends from one into the other.",
  "tutMercuryS3": "A hard board: more counts are hidden. When stuck, suppose a cell is filled and fill back to its bulb: if a row or column overflows or can no longer reach its count, that cell stays empty.",
  "exMercurySupposeFull": "Suppose {cell} were filled.",
  "@exMercurySupposeFull": {"placeholders": {"cell": {}}},
  "exMercurySupposeEmpty": "Suppose {cell} stayed empty.",
  "@exMercurySupposeEmpty": {"placeholders": {"cell": {}}},
