  "nightfallName": "Nightfall",
  "nightfallTagline": "Tap the windows until the whole house goes dark",
  "nightfallRules": "• Every cell is a window, lit or dark.\n• Tapping a window flips it and its neighbours above, below, left and right: lit windows go dark, dark ones light up.\n• Make every window dark, within the move limit if the game has one.\n\nThe order of taps never matters, and tapping a window twice changes nothing. When the edges wrap around, a window on the edge also flips the one across the board.",
  "nightfallEdges": "Edges",
  "nightfallEdgesFlat": "Flat",
  "nightfallEdgesFlatHint": "Windows on the edge have fewer neighbours.",
  "nightfallEdgesWrap": "Wrap around",
  "nightfallEdgesWrapHint": "The edges join up: a window on the edge also flips the one across the board.",
  "nightfallGoalMoves": "Move limit",
  "nightfallGoalMovesHint": "Make it dark within a few moves more than the fewest (none more on hard).",
  "nightfallGoalFree": "Free play",
  "nightfallGoalFreeHint": "No limit: take as many moves as you need.",
  "nightfallMoves": "{count, plural, one{{count} move} other{{count} moves}}",
  "@nightfallMoves": {"placeholders": {"count": {"type": "int"}}},
  "nightfallFewest": "Dark in the fewest moves!",
  "tutNightfall1": "A tap flips a window and its neighbours above, below, left and right: lit windows go dark, dark ones light up. Tap the middle window to make every window dark.",
  "tutNightfall2": "A window on the edge or in a corner has fewer neighbours, so a tap there flips fewer windows. Make every window dark.",
  "tutNightfall3": "Two taps that flip the same window leave it as it was. The order of taps never matters, and tapping a window twice changes nothing, so each window needs one tap or none.",
  "tutNightfall4": "A real board. The counter shows your moves and the limit: a few more than the fewest that make every window dark. Undo gives a move back.",
  "tutNightfallS1": "Chase the lights down: for each lit window in the top row, tap the window right below it, and the top row goes dark. Do the same for each row in turn, down to the bottom.",
  "tutNightfallS2": "Here a chase leaves lit windows in the bottom row: the top row needs a tap too. Tap the marked window first, then chase the lights down again.",
  "tutNightfallS3": "A real board with no move limit. Chase the lights down; if some stay lit in the bottom row, start over with a tap or two in the top row and chase again, until the bottom row goes dark too.",
  "tagSwitch": "lights, switch, toggle, on and off, dark, night, windows, flip, invert",
  "glideName": "Glide",
  "glideTagline": "Slide the tiles through the gap until they are back in order",
  "glideRules": "• The frame holds numbered tiles and one gap.\n• Tap a tile next to the gap to slide it in. Tapping a tile further along the gap's row or column slides it and every tile between at once. You can also swipe, or use the arrow keys.\n• Put the tiles in order: 1, 2, 3, … along each row from the top, with the gap in the bottom right corner.\n\nEvery tile that moves counts as a move. With colored tiles, rebuild the gradient instead: each tile belongs where its color fits.",
  "glideFace": "Tiles",
  "glideFaceNumbers": "Numbers",
  "glideFaceNumbersHint": "Numbered tiles, 1 in the top left corner.",
  "glideFaceColors": "Colors",
  "glideFaceColorsHint": "Tiles of a gradient: put each color where it fits.",
  "glideGoalFree": "Free play",
  "glideGoalFreeHint": "No limit: take as many moves as you need.",
  "glideGoalMoves": "Move limit",
  "glideGoalMovesHint": "Finish within a limit: twice the best way out on easy, 1.5 times on medium, 1.25 times on hard.",
  "glideMoves": "{count, plural, one{{count} move} other{{count} moves}}",
  "@glideMoves": {"placeholders": {"count": {"type": "int"}}},
  "glideFewest": "In order in the fewest moves!",
  "tutGlide1": "The frame has one gap. Tap the marked tile next to it to slide it into the gap. The goal: the tiles in order, 1, 2, 3, … row by row, with the gap at the end.",
  "tutGlide2": "A tile further along the gap's row or column slides too: tap the marked tile, and it moves along with every tile between it and the gap.",
  "tutGlide3": "You can also swipe a tile toward the gap, or swipe anywhere to slide the tile that can go that way. Swipe the marked tile up, then the tile below it.",
  "tutGlide4": "Now a few tiles are out of place. Put them back in order: tiles only ever move into the gap, so think about where the gap has to go.",
  "tutGlide5": "A real board. The counter shows your moves and the limit; every tile that moves counts. Undo gives moves back.",
  "tutGlideS1": "Big boards go row by row: put 1, 2, 3 and 4 into the top row, then leave that row alone. What is left is a smaller board; solve it the same way.",
  "tutGlideS2": "The end of a row is the tricky part: 1, 2 and 3 are home, but 4 can't get past 3 without moving it. Put 4 where 3 belongs first and 3 right below it, then slide both in together: 4 to the right, 3 up.",
  "tutGlideS3": "A real board. Place the top row, then the left column, then the next row, so the board keeps shrinking. The last two tiles of a column go in together, like the end of a row turned on its side. A small corner is left at the end: circle its tiles round until they fall into place.",
  "layersName": "Layers",
  "layersTagline": "Strokes over strokes: the last one shows",
  "layersRules": "• Every row and every column was painted once, from end to end, in the color of the stroke at its start.\n• The strokes came one after another, in an order you don't know. A later stroke covers an earlier one, so each cell has the color of whichever of its row and column came last.\n• Some cells are shown from the start (with a dot). Paint the rest.\n• A stroke with a question mark hides its color: work it out.\n\nPick a color in the palette and tap cells to paint them (tap again to clear), or tap a cell to cycle through the colors. Slide across cells to paint them all.",
  "tutLayers1": "Every row and every column was painted once, end to end, in the color of the stroke at its start. The strokes came one after another, and the last one over a cell is the one you see. When a cell's row and column have the same color, the cell can only be that color. Pick a color in the palette and paint the empty cells.",
  "tutLayers2": "A shown cell tells you which of its two strokes came later. A1 has row 1's color, so row 1 was painted after column A. A3 has column A's color: column A came after row 3. C3 has row 3's: row 3 came after column C. So row 1 came after column C too, and C1 gets row 1's color. Finish the board.",
  "tutLayers3": "A stroke with a question mark hides its color. A1 doesn't have column A's color, so row 1 was painted after column A, and A1 shows row 1's color. Now B1's row and column have the same color. Finish the board.",
  "tutLayers4": "Now a real board. Paint the cells whose row and column share a color, then follow the order around rectangles of shown cells.",
  "tutLayersS1": "The order also runs through cells that don't show it. If row 2 came after column B and column B after row 5, then row 2 came after row 5, even if no cell says so. Chains like that settle cells no single rectangle reaches. Some strokes hide their color here: read it from a cell where that line came last.",
  "tutLayersS2": "When nothing else helps, try a color: suppose a hidden stroke had one color, or a cell one of its two, and follow the order until it runs in a circle (a stroke would come after itself) or a cell has no color left. Then it's the other way.",
